# render_window_export.scn — live tooth for the Render-to-file window (port of TiXL RenderWindow.cs):
# the toolbar Render toggle opens a settings window that drives an app::ExportSession ONE frame per
# editor frame (TiXL RenderProcess.Update, cs:189-232). This scenario opens the window, selects the
# PNG-sequence codec, starts an export at the default range, and asserts — via the state.json
# renderExport hook (main.cpp) — that the per-frame pump ran the WHOLE range to completion.
#
# WHY state.json (not a file assert): the export writes a PNG directory, but the robust machine signal
# is the session's own progress (framesTotal / framesDone / done), which the shell serializes into
# state.json every frame. That couples the assertion to the actual export state machine, not to disk
# timing. The two-driver byte-parity + file existence + frame count are covered headless by
# --selftest-render-session (Leg C compares stepwise vs blocking PNGs byte-for-byte) and
# --selftest-export (Leg C: runExport → PNG sequence, files on disk).
#
# RED proofs:
#   - break drawRenderWindow's per-frame pump (drop the stepOneFrame call) -> the export never
#     advances -> renderExport.done stays false after the wait -> the done==true assert goes RED.
#   - break the Start button wiring (startExport never called) -> renderExport stays null -> the
#     framesTotal==60 assert goes RED.
#   - break the frame-range compute (wrong begin/end) -> framesTotal != 60 -> RED.

open app/testdata/compound_smoke.swproj

# --- 1. The Render toggle exists on the toolbar (eye recordItem toggle_render) ---
assert_map_has toggle_render
echo render-toggle-present

# Before opening the window no export has started -> the state hook reads null.
assert_state '.renderExport == null'
echo render-export-idle-null

# --- 2. Open the Render window; its controls appear (Start button + codec combo) ---
do click @toggle_render
wait 0.4
assert_map_has render_start_button
assert_map_has render_codec
echo render-window-open-controls-present

# --- 3. Select the PNG-sequence codec (row index 2 in the codec table: ProRes=0, H264=1, PNG=2).
# PNG writes a per-frame directory (the writer mkdir's it), so the export needs no pre-created .mov. ---
do click @render_codec
wait 0.3
do click @render_codec:2
wait 0.3
echo png-codec-selected

# --- 4. Shrink the range to 5 frames (End = 4, in the default Frames unit → 0..4 inclusive) so the
# export completes quickly regardless of the harness frame rate. InputDouble is a click-to-edit field;
# select-all (Cmd+A, Mac imgui maps Cmd→io.KeyCtrl) before typing because the hand's `text` APPENDS. ---
do click @render_end
wait 0.2
do keychord cmd a
do text 4
do key enter
wait 0.3
echo range-end-set-to-4

# --- 5. Start the export. The window pumps one frame per editor frame; the state hook reports
# framesTotal (5 = 0..4 inclusive) immediately after Start. ---
do click @render_start_button
wait 0.4
assert_state '.renderExport.framesTotal == 5'
echo export-started-5-frames

# --- 6. Let the per-frame pump run the whole range. 5 frames complete in well under a second of
# editor time even at a throttled harness frame rate; 3s is a safe ceiling. Then the session is done. ---
wait 3.0
assert_state '.renderExport.done == true'
echo export-ran-to-completion
assert_state '.renderExport.framesDone == 5'
echo export-wrote-all-5-frames
assert_state '.renderExport.cancelled == false'
echo export-completed-not-cancelled
